ai-3.0/dashboard.md`) and no new component or\ncontainer image is introduced.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 — four audited flows fully operable by keyboard, no focus traps or unreachable controls | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Primary scope of the Dashboard remediation epic. |\n| HLR-2 — visible focus indicators consistent across the audited flows | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Bundled with HLR-1: both HLRs are remediated by a single pass over the same four flows in the same component and team — the audit's defect list interleaves focus traps, unreachable controls, and inconsistent indicators per flow, so splitting would force two passes over the same UI surfaces and an artificial serialization edge between epics building the same surface. |\n| HLR-3 — conformance documentation (VPAT and accessibility statement) updated, republished, with technical review | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 | Substantive content authoring in the Documentation component — separate work stream and review cycle from the code fixes. Priority P2 per the docs-authoring exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Normal`). |\n\nAll three HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Component Mapping\n\n`Dashboard` and `Documentation` are both verbatim canonical names from\n`.context/rhai-components.txt` — no non-obvious mappings.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n  Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then) section, and no\n  Non-Functional Requirements section. Decomposition proceeded from the HLR\n  table and problem statement. The Dashboard owning team (RHOAI Dashboard) was\n  taken from `architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`; the Documentation epic's\n  team (RHOI Documentation is not established by any source — \"RHOAI\n  Documentation\" is an assumption) should be confirmed at plan review.\n- **AC derivation**: with no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria in the\n  strategy, epic ACs were derived from the HLRs and the audit's enumerated\n  defect categories. The audit report itself (referenced but not attached) is\n  the authoritative defect list and should be attached to the implementation\n  epic in Jira.\n- **Customer-driven deadline risk**: two public-sector customers require the\n  republished VPAT before renewal — a schedule pressure on the chain, not a\n  structural constraint; it does not change the DAG.\n- No priority inversions detected (P1 code remediation precedes P2\n  documentation, consistent with Rule 11 ordering).\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing; the strategy has\nno dependencies table and no open questions.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":23747,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a3258536ce2e6f27","agent_id":"adc00a8c258f6d0a3","session_id":"5d78f521-7e82-4c71-8bdb-ec850ccb8e22","uuid":"0a3829cc-3327-4080-81f5-d69476c6a532","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:20:07.409Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9017"}
