{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b2af82f813d01fd2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9017\n3\tepic_count: 2\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9017\n11\t\n12\tKeyboard accessibility conformance for core Dashboard flows.\n13\t\n14\t## Triage\n15\t\n16\tNot below-threshold and not docs-only: the strategy spans two components and two\n17\tarchitecturally distinct work streams — code remediation in the Dashboard (four\n18\taudited flows) and substantive content authoring in Documentation (VPAT and\n19\taccessibility statement update and republish). Full decomposition follows.\n20\t\n21\t## Epic List\n22\t\n23\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n24\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Keyboard accessibility remediation for core Dashboard flows | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 | Update and republish accessibility conformance documentation (VPAT and accessibility statement) | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P2 |\n27\t\n28\t## Dependency DAG\n29\t\n30\t```mermaid\n31\tgraph TD\n32\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 Keyboard accessibility remediation] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 Conformance documentation update]\n33\t```\n34\t\n35\t## DAG Justification\n36\t\n37\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n38\t|------|------|-----------|\n39\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics) | The VPAT and accessibility statement describe the fixed state, so the documentation cannot be authored or technically reviewed until the keyboard remediation is complete. |\n40\t\n41\t**Parallel non-edges:** none — with a single implementation epic there is no\n42\tparallelizable pair. No konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or\n43\tlicense-validation epic is generated: Dashboard is an existing, Konflux-onboarded\n44\tcomponent (see `architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`) and no new component or\n45\tcontainer image is introduced.\n46\t\n47\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n48\t\n49\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n50\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n51\t| HLR-1 — four audited flows fully operable by keyboard, no focus traps or unreachable controls | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Primary scope of the Dashboard remediation epic. |\n52\t| HLR-2 — visible focus indicators consistent across the audited flows | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Bundled with HLR-1: both HLRs are remediated by a single pass over the same four flows in the same component and team — the audit's defect list interleaves focus traps, unreachable controls, and inconsistent indicators per flow, so splitting would force two passes over the same UI surfaces and an artificial serialization edge between epics building the same surface. |\n53\t| HLR-3 — conformance documentation (VPAT and accessibility statement) updated, republished, with technical review | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 | Substantive content authoring in the Documentation component — separate work stream and review cycle from the code fixes. Priority P2 per the docs-authoring exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Normal`). |\n54\t\n55\tAll three HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n56\t\n57\t## Component Mapping\n58\t\n59\t`Dashboard` and `Documentation` are both verbatim canonical names from\n60\t`.context/rhai-components.txt` — no non-obvious mappings.\n61\t\n62\t## Health Warnings\n63\t\n64\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n65\t  Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then) section, and no\n66\t  Non-Functional Requirements section. Decomposition proceeded from the HLR\n67\t  table and problem statement. The Dashboard owning team (RHOAI Dashboard) was\n68\t  taken from `architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`; no source establishes the\n69\t  Documentation epic's owner, so \"RHOAI Documentation\" is an assumption that\n70\t  should be confirmed at plan review.\n71\t- **AC derivation**: with no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria in the\n72\t  strategy, epic ACs were derived from the HLRs and the audit's enumerated\n73\t  defect categories. The audit report itself (referenced but not attached) is\n74\t  the authoritative defect list and should be attached to the implementation\n75\t  epic in Jira.\n76\t- **E001 size / conditional re-split trigger**: the epic is one logical change\n77\t  (a single mechanical sweep of the audit's enumerated defect list over the four\n78\t  audited flows, same component and team) sized at roughly the two-week line, so\n79\t  it is not split now. Re-split trigger: if remediation of any single flow\n80\t  exceeds 3 engineering days, or the sweep as a whole exceeds 10 engineering\n81\t  days, split the epic along flow boundaries (one epic per audited flow, or\n82\t  flows grouped under that budget), since the four flows are independent UI\n83\t  surfaces and can be remediated in parallel once the defect list is enumerated\n84\t  per flow.\n85\t- **Customer-driven deadline risk**: two public-sector customers require the\n86\t  republished VPAT before renewal — a schedule pressure on the chain, not a\n87\t  structural constraint; it does not change the DAG.\n88\t- No priority inversions detected (P1 code remediation precedes P2\n89\t  documentation, consistent with Rule 11 ordering).\n90\t\n91\t## Tiered Delivery\n92\t\n93\tNot applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing; the strategy has\n94\tno dependencies table and no open questions.\n95\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-887b2e358a0d4f85","agent_id":"a6e150e2033ee2355","session_id":"5d78f521-7e82-4c71-8bdb-ec850ccb8e22","uuid":"423b87ee-acfe-4961-98e8-3817c42ab58a","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:26:12.014Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b2af82f813d01fd2","permission_decision":{"decision":